module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.silent_first_wins_rebind_of_a_generic_type_variable_in_unify_generics

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data silent_first_wins_rebind_of_a_generic_type_variable_in_unify_generics: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "silent_first_wins_rebind_of_a_generic_type_variable_in_unify_generics" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "INVALID STATE: unify_generics folds a call's actuals onto one accumulator and its already-bound arm is `if map_has(acc.subst, g) { acc }` — when a parameter already carries a binding, the later actual is DISCARDED and nothing is said, so a call whose arguments instantiate the same formal type variable to two different types is ACCEPTED on the first binding alone. The caller then receives a declared return type instantiated with a binding that no argument of the call implies. This is a fail-open: the compiler knows the two actuals disagree, prints nothing, and keeps typing the call as if the first one were the whole story (DESIGN section 5, an error must not masquerade as success).",

    "THE SAME SHAPE HID THE DEFECT THAT MADE IT MEASURABLE. The producer repair in the #14152 lane (src/v1/04_infer.dag call arm, commit 81824abfa3) is what first produced a corpus whose Map-returning generic calls disagree about K and V: before it, the Map-family rebinds compared a call's type against the callee's OWN formal parameters, because an argument-less generic call left the instantiation map empty and `substitute_generics_apply` short-circuits on an empty map, handing the declaration's own K/V to the caller as if instantiated. HOW MANY is not quoted: the census that counted them is a lane instrument that is not part of this change, and DESIGN section 6 makes a number citable only by the producer that re-derives it. Those rows were invisible to this class precisely because the fold was silent: one silent defect was masking another.",

    "MEASUREMENT, AND WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT QUOTED HERE. Reporting the conflict turns main's silence into hard type mismatches across the emitted closures where main produces none, and those mismatches are of three kinds, defined mechanically by what the diagnostic shows a reader: (1) GENUINE DISAGREEMENT -- the bound side and the later actual are different types, which is this row's class; (2) the binding being rebound renders as a KNOWN FABRICATED PLACEHOLDER, `Product(Unit<>)` or an empty `Primitive()`, which blames a call site for a slot that was never a type anyone wrote and belongs to `gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.error_type_fabricated_as_a_type_by_total_accessors`; (3) IDENTICAL RENDERING -- expected and got print the same string, so the reader sees 'expected X, got X' and cannot tell a deliberate refusal from a broken comparator. NO COUNTS ARE QUOTED, for the reason DESIGN section 6 gives: a number is citable only by a producer that re-derives it, and the census that classified these rows is a lane instrument that is not part of this change, so transcribing its tallies here would leave numbers that rot unreachably. Anyone needing the split re-derives it by running the two-closure comparison over the head that carries the conflict report; the three definitions above are what makes that run reproducible rather than a re-typing of a table.",

    "CONSEQUENCE FOR A BUILD, FROM CODE RATHER THAN FROM A LOG. The conflict reuses the shared `type_mismatch_error`, which is SeverityError; `is_interpreter_blocking_diagnostic` (src/v1/stage0/src/v1_std_core.rs) maps TypeMismatch to Blocking, and the compile-clean gate (src/v1/stage0/src/cli_run.rs, with `compile_clean_diagnostic_is_hard` in cli_run/compile_clean.rs) refuses to emit while a blocking diagnostic stands. So a corpus containing such a call cannot be built while the conflict is reported -- and the first repair is NOT to soften the report: main's green build is green BECAUSE of the fail-open, so any fix must remove the disagreement rather than its visibility. The per-run counts (how many modules, how many diagnostics, whether emission was refused) are observable on any CI run of the head that carries the report and are deliberately not transcribed here; PR-2's acceptance gate is the entry point that re-derives them.",

    "REPAIR STATUS, stated against the commit that carries this row rather than against the lane. NOT YET ON MAIN: the report itself -- `UnifyGenericsAcc { subst, diagnostics }`, which turns the already-bound arm into a decision and emits the conflict-path diagnostic, with the RED `generic_type_variable_rebind_conflict_is_reported_when_a_bound_variable_gets_a_different_type` and an arm-naming mutation control. It is deliberately NOT in this change: reporting the conflict makes the corpus refuse emission (measured at the time of writing: hard type mismatches appear in both emitted closures where main has none, and emission is refused on the head while main emits its seed modules; the counts are re-derivable by PR-2's gate and are not transcribed here), so a PR carrying both the report and the producer repair can only be green by softening the report. What THIS change carries is NOT a repair of the producer. It carries a permanent BOUNDARY GUARD, `a_call_that_passes_actuals_gets_no_call_site_mint` (src/v1/tests/src/call_site_mint_boundary_test.rs): a generic call that passes actuals and leaves a declared parameter unbound must not be handed a minted variable, because absence from `call_subst` at such a call means `unify_generics` failed to bind the parameter and minting would fabricate a binding -- the same silent-wrong-binding class this row names. The mint itself (fresh per-call-site ids at the ExprCall call arm, `fresh_call_variables`, narrowed to argument-less calls) WAS built and measured, and is deliberately NOT shipped: the emitter cannot carry a minted variable in any id form, so the producer defect this row describes remains OPEN on main, where an argument-less generic call still hands the callee's own formal parameters to the caller as if instantiated. Read this row as a documented class plus a pinned boundary, not as a closed defect. RUNG OF THAT DISCRIMINATOR, STATED SO IT IS NOT OVER-CLAIMED: it is a v1 Rust unit test, and the v1 unit-test lane is off the required path by design -- `gunbc.rung_drop.rust_unit_tests_off_the_merge_path` and DESIGN 'What a green v2-bootstrap does not say' record that no required job runs it. So the boundary this row pins is proven by an EXECUTED input whose execution is dispatched by hand, not by a required lane that would go red on its own -- and the guard PASSES TODAY BECAUSE NOTHING MINTS, which is why its value is prospective: it goes red the moment a blocked mint is landed and widened, and it is written with guards that fail if its fixture ever stops reaching the arm; making the invariant visible to a required lane needs a claim-DSL helper that reports minted type-variable ids, and none exists: the helpers the claim DSL exposes report emit equality, diagnostic census counts, call edges, and leaf guards, not type-variable identity. The mint id is not unreachable in principle -- `named_type_vars_in_node` and `render_named_type_base` (src/v1/05_emit.dag) render an unbound variable's id as a rendered type argument -- but no required assertion reads it, so a required guard over this invariant is a new carrier with its own RED, not a line in this change. So the row is filed as documentation of an open class plus a provably-green boundary guard; the producer repair is a separate change blocked on the emitter item above, and the report follows as its own change once the corpus clears under it; the conflict-path diagnostic named above is that change's RED, and the EmissionRefused it produces at a seed module is its gate. NOT PLANNED AS PART OF EITHER: the refine-don't-fix rule (an uninformative binding -- a TypeVariable, or a Product(Unit<>) placeholder -- REFINED by an informative one instead of fixing the slot). The census described above satisfies the pre-registered predicate for building it, but the two upstream classes named above (fabricated placeholders, identical renderings) are its prerequisites, and it must be measured against a baseline that can already see the conflict -- so after the report, not beside it.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: the type-unification authority (a total fold that drops information and reports nothing). CEILING: the fold's already-bound arm becomes a decision — refine, refuse, or keep — and each choice needs its own control, so the ceiling is reached once the report exists and is not exceeded by softening it. NOT SCHEDULED, AS A BOUNDED OPERATOR DECISION: the disposition of the fabricated-placeholder rows and the identical-rendering rows; the decision is scoped to this lane. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, NAMING THE CAPABILITY: the refine-don't-fix rule shared by unify_generics and unify_lambda_solves, admitted once the report is on main, so that a rule change can be measured against a baseline that can already see the conflict.",

    "FRAGILITY RECORDED, NOT FIXED (1), and the boundary where it belongs: `unify_generics` records NO binding when an actual's relevant leaf is the CALLER'S OWN type variable. Measured consequence, on a real corpus call: `fn witness_measured_rollup_differs_from_predicted_zero<S>` (dag/std/realization_measurement.dag) passes a `Measure<Time, S, Nat>` actual whose leaf is the caller's own `S`; no binding is recorded, so a caller-side test of 'is this parameter already instantiated' is false for a parameter the actuals should have settled. Treating that absence as 'uninstantiable' and minting a fresh variable is a FABRICATION -- it replaces a variable the caller named with one no actual mentions, which is a silent wrong binding, the class this row is about. The mint that was built therefore applied only to a call carrying no argument nodes at all; a call that passes actuals keeps main's behaviour, and that boundary is pinned permanently by the row `a_call_that_passes_actuals_gets_no_call_site_mint` (src/v1/tests/src/call_site_mint_boundary_test.rs), so a later widening of the mint -- whenever it is unblocked -- cannot cross it quietly. The repair of the absence itself lives in `unify_generics` (an uninformative binding should be refined, not fixed, and that rule is shared with `unify_lambda_solves`), not here, and it is routed as Charter 1's question -- not fixed by this row's author.",

    "FRAGILITY RECORDED, NOT FIXED (2), AND IT IS THE BLOCKER FOR THE DEFERRED MINT: the emitter derives a type variable's IDENTITY from its NAME at three sites, so any variable that is not under a name the compiler can recompute loses rendering, its Clone bound, and its clone-bound pre-pass -- silently. Site 1, rendering: src/v1/05_emit.dag:852, inside the type-argument arm, renders a type argument only when `tn != \"\" && tn == var_id` -- the written name must equal the variable's id -- so a minted id that is not an authored name is not emitted as a generic argument at all, and the position collapses to whatever the expression defaulted to. Site 2, the fold-accumulator bridge: src/v1/05_emit_rust.dag:14098 emits `HashMap::new() /* BRIDGE: fold empty_map accumulator type unresolved */` when a fold accumulator's map type cannot be resolved, so an unbound map variable is substituted by a placeholder comment rather than reported. Site 3, the Clone bound: `emit_item_header_clone_param_names` and `v1_item_wf_propagated_clone_bounded_param_names` (src/v1/05_emit_rust.dag) return declared parameter NAMES, and `emit_type_params_with_clone_bound` (src/v1/05_emit_rust.dag:7991) appends `: Clone` by comparing each param's name against them -- `if pname == clone_param { concat(pascal, \": Clone\") } else { pascal }` -- so a variable that is not under a declared name gets no bound and nothing is reported. Measured: the SAME emitted function carries the bound under a name-keyed mint and loses it under a call-site-keyed mint (`pub fn witness_measured_rollup_differs_from_predicted_zero<S: Clone>` against `<S>`), the compiler stays mute, and it surfaces only when cargo builds the emitted product, as `error[E0277]: the trait bound <name>: Clone is not satisfied`; with the rendering site blocked instead, the same class surfaces as `error: UNRESOLVED_CompilerError` in a generated `HashMap::<compile_error!(\"UNRESOLVED_CompilerError\"), compile_error!(\"UNRESOLVED_CompilerError\")>::new()` for a `fold(init: empty_map(), ...)` accumulator (src/v2/compiler/symbol_index_fill.dag:94). Both were found only by `gunbc test //gunbc/instruments:self-host` on the emitted product, which is why it is now in the pre-push gate. DISPOSITION: the mint is its own change, BLOCKED on the emitter keying rendering and clone bounds on a stable variable identity rather than a name -- three sites, one property. Charter 6 owns that item. Until it lands, no per-call-site mint can be shipped in any id form, and this row's producer defect stays open. "
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
